[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be an orange open-access style icon/logo on a white background. It does not contain scientific panels, data plots, biological objects, experimental labels, model interfaces, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small circular app-style icon or badge. It contains a multicolored ring and a simple central shape, but no scientific panels, labels, biological source objects, transformations, model interfaces, plots, microscopy, or experimental findings are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 3, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about CellWhisperer, a joint embedding/model interface connecting transcriptomes and text.\n\nPanel a: Schematic workflow. Left shows dataset construction from “1 million pairs of human transcriptomes and text,” generating sample descriptions using an LLM, with metadata, expert annotations, GEO metadata, RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, gene expression counts, and generated conversations. Center shows training and inference in a “multimodal embedding space (2,048 dimensions)” with language model adapter, transcriptome model adapter, contrastive alignment/minimize distance, supervised training, and generative LLM/Mistral interface. Right shows applications: free-text cell labeling from embedded dataset with prompt “immune cells,” term matching via embedding similarity, CellWhisperer score, and chat about user-provided transcriptome data, including question-answer bubbles about scRNA-seq dataset identity and characteristic genes/pathways.\n\nPanel b: UMAP landscape titled “Landscape of annotated human transcriptomes,” showing many colored clusters. Caption indicates CellWhisperer embedding of 705,430 transcriptomes from GEO.\n\nPanel c: UMAP query result titled “Querying the human transcriptome landscape with natural language,” showing red/blue CellWhisperer scores for a search term related to “inflammation.” Color bar labeled CellWhisperer score, roughly from negative blue to positive red. Dashed callouts point to regions matching textual query phrases such as inflamed immune response, heightened immune response to MTB infection, K562 leukemia cells, and active remodeling/immune-response cells.\n\nPanel d: Three histogram plots titled “GEO submission dates for the selected samples,” with x-axis GEO submission date and y-axis number of samples. The three rows correspond to selected query-associated sample groups, including CD34+ HSPCs with broad differentiation potential, K562 leukemia cells cultured in supplemented RPMI 1640, and active remodeling and immune-response cells."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 4, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific schematic/evaluation figure about single-cell RNA-seq cell type annotation.\n\nVisible content:\n- Left panel: An “Expert-annotated scRNA-seq evaluation dataset (not used during training)” shown as a UMAP embedding with many colored cell clusters.\n- A red-labeled cell population “Erythrocytes” is highlighted on the left side of the UMAP.\n- Axes are labeled “UMAP.”\n- Middle: A “Free-text query for cell types” arrow points from the annotated dataset toward the evaluation output.\n- Right panel: “Evaluation against expert-annotated cell types.”\n- Inside the right box: a query example, “Query for ‘erythrocytes’.”\n- A UMAP visualization shows cells colored by “CellWhisperer score,” with a vertical color scale.\n- The predicted/high-scoring region is labeled “Erythrocytes” in red.\n- Bottom-right: A density/histogram plot of “CellWhisperer score,” comparing “Other cell types” in gray versus “Erythrocytes” in red.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell RNA-seq cells.\n- Expert-annotated cell types.\n- Erythrocytes as the queried/evaluated cell type.\n\nTransformation/model interface:\n- Expert-labeled scRNA-seq data are queried using a free-text cell type prompt.\n- The model/interface returns CellWhisperer scores over cells.\n- Scores are visualized on UMAP and summarized with a density/histogram distribution.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Cells annotated as erythrocytes receive higher CellWhisperer scores than other cell types, indicating the query “erythrocytes” retrieves the corresponding cell population."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 4, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a scientific figure panel labeled **b** about **zero-shot prediction performance for common cell types**.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Two left UMAP scatter plots:\n  - Top: **Tabula Sapiens annotation**\n  - Bottom: **CellWhisperer prediction**\n  - Both show colored clusters of single cells in UMAP space with gray background points and multiple colored annotated cell-type groups.\n- Right panel: a confusion-matrix-style heatmap titled **“Tabula Sapiens (20 common cell types)”** comparing:\n  - Rows: expert/Tabula Sapiens annotated cell classes\n  - Columns: **CellWhisperer prediction**\n  - Color scale: **Predicted cells per expert-annotated class (%)**, from 0 to 80.\n- Cell-type labels include:\n  - Erythrocyte, macrophage, monocyte, non-classical monocyte, classical monocyte, neutrophil, basophil, NK cell, CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell, CD4-positive alpha-beta memory T cell, CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell, CD8-positive alpha-beta cytokine secreting effector T cell, naive B cell, memory B cell, capillary endothelial cell, type II pneumocyte, respiratory goblet cell, club cell, basal cell, hepatocyte.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell data from **Tabula Sapiens**\n- Human cell types across immune, epithelial, endothelial, and other lineages.\n\nModel/interface shown:\n- **CellWhisperer** zero-shot prediction output compared against Tabula Sapiens expert annotations.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- The heatmap shows strongest signal mostly along the diagonal, indicating that CellWhisperer predictions often match expert-annotated cell classes, with some off-diagonal confusion among related cell types."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 2 | Benchmarking of the CellWhisperer embedding model through zero-shot prediction of cell characteristics. a , Conceptual outline of the performance evaluation for zero-shot prediction of cell types in a zero-shot manner. Left, UMAP visualization of CellWhisperer embeddings for all cells in the Tabula Sapiens dataset, colored by the dataset's expert-annotated cell types (as a ground truth). Right, CellWhisperer scores for the free-text query 'erythrocytes' projected on the UMAP (top) and as histograms for expert-annotated erythrocytes versus other cell types (bottom). b , Comparison of expertannotated cell types (UMAP, top left) and CellWhisperer predictions (UMAP, bottom left) for the Tabula Sapiens (20 common cell types) dataset, with the confusion matrix shown as a heat map (right). The blue color gradient indicates", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is panel **c** of a scientific figure showing grouped bar-chart benchmarks for **cell type prediction**.\n\nVisible content:\n- Title: **“Benchmarking of cell type prediction”**\n- Three metric rows on the y-axis:\n  - **Accuracy**\n  - **F1 score**\n  - **AUROC**\n- Five benchmark datasets on the x-axis:\n  - **Tabula Sapiens**\n  - **Tabula Sapiens (20 common cell types)**\n  - **Pancreas**\n  - **ImmGen**\n  - **Asian Immune Diversity Atlas**\n- Two training regimes separated by horizontal grouping labels on the right:\n  - **Fine-tuned**\n  - **Zero-shot**\n- Legend labeled **Model**, comparing:\n  - **Geneformer unfrozen, with pseudo-bulking**\n  - **Geneformer frozen, without pseudo-bulking**\n  - **Geneformer frozen, with pseudo-bulking**\n  - **CellWhisperer embedding model**\n  - **CellAssign method**\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell or cell atlas datasets used for cell type annotation / prediction benchmarking.\n- Named biological datasets include human Tabula Sapiens, pancreas, mouse immune ImmGen, and Asian Immune Diversity Atlas.\n\nModel/interface content:\n- The figure compares cell type prediction performance across models using Geneformer variants, CellWhisperer embeddings, and CellAssign.\n- Geneformer conditions vary by whether the model is frozen or unfrozen and whether pseudo-bulking is used.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Performance varies strongly by dataset, metric, and model.\n- **CellWhisperer embedding model** and **CellAssign method** often show higher bars than Geneformer variants in several settings.\n- Geneformer variants generally appear lower in many zero-shot and fine-tuned comparisons, though exact values differ by dataset and metric.\n- AUROC values are generally higher than Accuracy and F1 score across many datasets."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 4, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart labeled panel **d**. It reports **zero-shot prediction performance for different cell characteristics**.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Three dataset groups across columns:\n  - **Tabula Sapiens dataset**: organ/tissue, **24 classes**\n  - **Human Diseases dataset**: tissue, **27 classes**\n  - **Human Diseases dataset**: disease subtype, **229 classes**\n- Two performance metrics across rows:\n  - **AUROC**\n  - **Accuracy**\n- Orange bars show performance for individual categories, with a black-outlined bar for **All (macro-averaged)**.\n- Dashed horizontal lines indicate **Random baseline**:\n  - Around 0.5 for AUROC\n  - Near 0 for accuracy, depending on class count\n\nVisible category labels:\n- Tabula Sapiens organ/tissue: **Tongue**, **Lung**, **Kidney**, **All (macro-averaged)**\n- Human Diseases tissue: **Heart**, **Liver**, **Ovary**, **All (macro-averaged)**\n- Human Diseases disease subtype: **Hepatocellular carcinoma**, **Dilated cardiomyopathy**, **Meningioma**, **All (macro-averaged)**\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human biological samples/cells grouped by organ, tissue, or disease subtype.\n- No microscopy images, molecular structures, cells, or tissue images are directly shown; the figure summarizes classification performance.\n\nModel/interface details:\n- The chart evaluates a **zero-shot prediction** model or method, but the model architecture, inputs, and interface are not shown in this cropped panel.\n\nFindings:\n- AUROC values are consistently above the random baseline across shown datasets and categories.\n- Accuracy varies by task and category.\n- Macro-averaged accuracy appears moderate for organ/tissue and tissue tasks, but much lower for disease subtype classification with 229 classes."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 6, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/scientific figure labeled **a-e** about **CellWhisperer** analysis of embryonic development and heart-related gene markers in **CS8 embryo** spatial expression data.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Heatmap showing “CellWhisperer captures key developmental stages” across **days after fertilization** and queried Carnegie/developmental stages. Rows include **CS1-2, CS3-5, CS6-8, CS9-23**. Columns are days after fertilization. A color scale labeled **CellWhisperer score (mean across cells per day)** ranges from blue/negative to red/positive. Top schematic marks developmental stages: **zygote, blastula, gastrula, organogenesis**. Text snippets at right show example CellWhisperer natural-language queries for embryonic stages.\n- **Panel b:** Heatmap strip for CellWhisperer query **“heart”**, showing scores over days after fertilization. A z-score color scale is shown, blue to red, labeled **CellWhisperer z score (mean across cells per day)**.\n- **Panel c:** Venn diagram and violin plots for “CellWhisperer identifies heart marker genes.” The Venn compares **CellWhisperer-identified genes**, **marker genes from fetal gene expression atlas**, and **random genes (control)**. Violin plots show number of papers co-mentioning **“heart”** and the gene name, with p-values annotated.\n- **Panel d:** Violin/strip plot titled **“Papers co-mentioning gene names and ‘heart’”**, grouped by expression enrichment in top 3% of cells for the **“heart”** query: **<0**, **0-1.5**, and **>1.5**. The high-enrichment group is highlighted and annotated with **“26 new marker genes.”**\n- **Panel e:** Spatial expression maps titled **“Spatial expression of ‘heart’ marker genes in CS8 embryo.”** Shows gene expression patterns for **IGDCC3** and **LRRTM3** as CellWhisperer-identified markers, established markers including **ISL1** and lateral plate mesoderm, and comparison genes **GAPDH** and **NOTO**.\n\nBiological source objects visible: embryonic developmental stages, CS8 embryo spatial transcriptomics/expression maps, heart marker genes, fetal gene expression atlas markers, gene-name literature co-mentions.\n\nModel/interface shown: **CellWhisperer** natural-language query interface using text prompts such as embryonic stage descriptions and **“heart”**, producing cell/day-level scores and gene marker identification.\n\nMain finding depicted: CellWhisperer scores align with developmental timing, the **“heart”** query identifies temporally and spatially relevant heart-associated cells/genes, and literature/enrichment analyses support candidate heart marker genes including newly highlighted markers."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 7, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure showing a CellWhisperer interface and analysis workflow for the Tabula Sapiens dataset.\n\nPanel a: Browser-style screenshot titled “CellWhisperer exploration of the Tabula Sapiens dataset.” It shows an interactive UMAP/scatter embedding colored by CellWhisperer score, with red and blue cell regions. A histogram at left shows score-based selection for the free-text query “structural cells with immune functions.” The interface includes annotated controls for cell search, score-based selection, visual selection, and natural-language chat. A chat panel on the right contains message blocks and a request input box.\n\nPanel b: Large UMAP-like cell embedding titled “Cell annotation with free-text query ‘structural cells with immune functions’.” Cells are colored by CellWhisperer score using a blue-to-red scale from approximately -8 to 8. Dashed outlines and text annotations identify biological cell/source groups including macrophages, endothelium, epithelial cells, ciliated epithelial cells from the nasal cavity, glial cells derived from ileum tissue, pericytes, stroma, epithelial cells from the endometrium of a fertile healthy female, and a selected fibroblast-like cluster described as from decidua tissue of a female individual at 10 weeks after conception.\n\nPanel c: Chat protocol panel showing user questions and CellWhisperer responses. The selected cells are described as fibroblasts derived from decidua tissue. Mentioned genes/pathways include COL1A1, COL3A1, COL1A2, MMP1, MMP2, HSPA1A, HSPB1, HSP90AA1, IFITM3, ISG15, JUN, and FOSB. The responses interpret the cells as extracellular-matrix remodeling fibroblasts with stress response, antiviral/immune-related signaling, and potential interaction with immune cells during pregnancy.\n\nPanel d: UMAP-like expression visualization titled “Mean expression of the genes mentioned by CellWhisperer.” A green-to-blue/purple log-scale expression color bar is shown, with a dashed selected cell cluster highlighted on the right side. The highlighted cluster has elevated mean expression relative to most other regions.\n\nOverall finding: The figure demonstrates CellWhisperer selecting and interpreting cells matching a natural-language biological query, then using chat responses and gene-expression visualization to support annotation of selected decidual fibroblast-like cells with immune-related and extracellular-matrix remodeling functions."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Fig. 5 | Interactive CellWhisperer-based and conventional bioinformatics analysis of a scRNA-seq dataset. a , Import and exploration of user-provided scRNA-seq data in CellWhisperer. b , UMAP of the CellWhisperer transcriptome embeddings for the imported Colonic Epithelium dataset 41 comprising scRNA-seq profiles of inflamed and noninflamed tissue biopsies of individuals with inflammatory bowel disease and healthy individuals. Cluster labels were generated by CellWhisperer and clusters were repositioned for compact visualization (interactive version: https://cellwhisperer.bocklab.org/colonic_ epithelium). c , Zoomed-in view of the cluster labeled 'Cycling ileal epithelial precursor cells', colored by CellWhisperer scores for the free-text query: 'Show me stem cells'. d , CellWhisperer chat about the top 100 cells with highest CellWhisperer score (query from c ). e , Expression levels of the LGR5 gene mentioned in the CellWhisperer response (in d ), plotted for the cell cluster from c . f , Histogram of CellWhisperer scores (query from c ) for cells derived from inflamed versus noninflamed tissue. g , Outline of a conventional bioinformatics", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure comparing a CellWhisperer natural-language single-cell RNA-seq analysis workflow with a conventional bioinformatics workflow.\n\nVisible panels and content:\n\n- **a**: Schematic workflow for CellWhisperer analysis. Input is user-provided scRNA-seq data in h5ad format, shown as a read count matrix with genes and cells. An automated processing pipeline produces a CellWhisperer-ready h5ad dataset with gene matrix, embeddings, and cluster labels. The output supports interactive exploration locally or via web server, including AI cluster labels, cell search, and cell chat. Example analysis is epithelial stem cells in health and inflammation.\n- **b**: UMAP plot titled “CellWhisperer-generated cluster labels.” Many colored cell clusters are annotated with text labels such as activated CD8+ cells in intestine, intestinal B cells, mast cells expressing inflammatory mediator genes, intestinal tuft cells, goblet-cell-related groups, colonocytes, enteroendocrine cells, proliferating transit-amplifying cells, and a highlighted label “Cycling ileal epithelial precursor cells.”\n- **c**: UMAP-like query score plot for CellWhisperer query “Show me stem cells,” colored by CellWhisperer score.\n- **d**: Chat interface panel about top-scoring “stem cells.” A green prompt asks: “Describe this subpopulation of cells in detail.” The response describes epithelial-layer terminal ileum cells, specifically an **LGR5-expressing stem cell subpopulation**, with genes involved in protein synthesis, cellular stress response, and epithelial cell function.\n- **e**: UMAP feature plot showing expression of the mentioned gene **LGR5**, with a color scale for gene expression.\n- **f**: Distribution plot of “stem cells” CellWhisperer score by inflammation status, comparing inflamed and noninflamed groups.\n- **g**: Conventional bioinformatics workflow schematic using tools/steps labeled scVI integration, CellTypist annotation, scanpy differential analysis, and matplotlib/pandas plotting.\n- **h**: Dataset integration UMAPs comparing raw and integrated embeddings, with groups labeled healthy, inflamed, and noninflamed.\n- **i**: Cluster-level cell type annotation UMAP with labels including B cells, T cells, TA cells, colonocytes, BEST4+ epithelial, and BEST2+ goblet cell.\n- **j**: Zoomed cell-level annotation panel highlighting a region labeled “Stem cells.”\n- **k**: Differential expression plot for “Stem cells,” showing ranked log2 fold changes. **LGR5** is marked significant.\n- **l**: Violin/box-style plot of “Stemness score” across cells by inflammation status, comparing inflamed vs noninflamed, with a significance marker.\n\nBiological source objects: single-cell RNA-seq cells from colonic/intestinal epithelial tissue, including epithelial stem cells, goblet cells, colonocytes, immune cells, and inflammation-status groups.\n\nTransformations shown: read count matrix processing, embedding generation, clustering, natural-language query scoring, gene expression visualization, dataset integration, cell type annotation, differential expression analysis, and functional score comparison.\n\nMain finding visually communicated: CellWhisperer can identify and describe an LGR5-associated epithelial stem-cell-like subpopulation from natural-language queries, producing results comparable to a conventional scRNA-seq analysis pipeline, including cell annotation, differential expression, and inflammation-associated stemness scoring."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 17, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow/model architecture figure for integrating transcriptomic data with language-model instruction tuning.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left input block: “Paired dataset: Transcriptomes & annotations,” listing datasets `GEO` with `705,430` data points and `CELLxGENE Census` with `376,983`.\n- Biological source objects: single-cell transcriptomes represented as “2048 top-expressed genes (tokenized)” and natural-language annotations/biomedical text.\n- Left model: `BioBERT`, labeled as pre-trained on biomedical texts, unfrozen, with `12 layers; 110M parameters`.\n- BioBERT output transformation: natural-language annotations are tokenized and passed through BioBERT to produce `768-dimensional` representations, then a `BatchNorm+ReLU` block maps `768→2048` and `2048→2048`, producing normalized `2048-dim.` embeddings.\n- Top-center model: `Geneformer`, labeled pre-trained on `30M single cells`, frozen, with `12 layers, 40M parameters`.\n- Geneformer output transformation: transcriptome gene tokens produce a `512-dimensional` embedding, then a `BatchNorm+ReLU` block maps `512→2048` and `2048→2048`, yielding normalized `2048-dim.` embeddings.\n- Center loss panel: matrix-style contrastive alignment using `InfoNCE loss`, with orange diagonal positive matches, gray negatives, labels such as “Dot product,” “Maximize,” “Minimize,” and batch size `512`.\n- Right input block: “Paired dataset: Transcriptomes & conversations,” listing conversation types and counts: detailed `81,610`, complex `10,000`, simple `5,000`.\n- Right model interface: transcriptome embeddings and question-answer conversations are tokenized and concatenated into an `8*4096-dim.` token embedding sequence.\n- Connector block: `2048→8*4096` and `8*4096→8*4096`, with `GELU`.\n- Right model: `Mistral 7B LLM (Instruct v0.2)`, described as pre-trained on proprietary text data and instruction-tuned on public data.\n- Training schedule labels: first epoch frozen, second epoch unfrozen.\n- Objective: multimodal instruction tuning with cross-entropy loss on answer tokens while question tokens are masked.\n\nNo experimental results or quantitative findings are shown beyond dataset sizes, model dimensions, parameters, and training/interface design."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 17, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains two scientific plot panels from a “Human Diseases dataset.”\n\nLeft panel: grouped bar chart showing “Recall at <top n>” on the y-axis and “Retrieved modality” on the x-axis, with two retrieved modalities: Target and Transcriptomes. Bars are shaded by top matches: 1, 5, 10, 50. Reported values:\n- Target: top 1 = 0.19, top 5 = 0.55, top 10 = 0.79, top 50 = 0.97\n- Transcriptomes: top 1 = 0.24, top 5 = 0.59, top 10 = 0.79, top 50 = 0.98\n\nRight panel: line plot for the same “Human Diseases dataset,” showing “Recall at top 5” versus “Epoch.” Recall rises sharply early, fluctuates around roughly 0.53-0.62, and ends near about 0.55-0.56 by epoch 15.\n\nBiological/source objects visible from labels: human disease-related targets and transcriptomes. No microscopy, molecular structures, pathways, or model architecture diagrams are shown."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 17, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a horizontal bar/strip plot comparing normalized scores across experimental settings. It appears to summarize machine learning model performance or ablation results.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Single plot panel.\n- X-axis labeled: “Normalized scores (fixed range 0-1)”, spanning 0.0 to 1.0.\n- Y-axis lists experimental conditions:\n  - “LiT: Both models frozen”\n  - “Loss function: JSD”\n  - “LiT: Only language model frozen”\n  - “LiT: Both models unfrozen”\n  - “Batch size: 32, Grad. accum.: 16”\n  - “Transcriptome model: scGPT”\n  - “Data: Only GEO”\n  - “Loss function: Mean(JSD, InfoNCE)”\n  - “Gradient accumulation: 16”\n  - “Data: CELLxGENE Census”\n  - “Augmentation: 2 annotation sets”\n  - “Sample weights: Constant (1)”\n  - “CellWhisperer embedding model”\n- Each row has a gray horizontal bar and overlaid colored points.\n- Points are shown in multiple colors, including green, blue, purple, pink, red, and orange.\n- The lowest row, “CellWhisperer embedding model,” is highlighted with an orange bar.\n- The plot likely compares multiple metrics or runs across model/data/training ablations, but the legend is not visible.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- Mentions transcriptomic/cell datasets and models: “scGPT,” “GEO,” and “CELLxGENE Census.”\n- The figure likely concerns single-cell or transcriptome embedding/model evaluation.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- Several configurations have gray bars around roughly 0.55-0.75 normalized score.\n- “CellWhisperer embedding model” has an orange highlighted bar around the lower-to-mid range, roughly near 0.6.\n- Colored points show substantial spread across rows, with some scores near 1.0 and others near 0.0 depending on condition/metric."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 18, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart comparing model performance across datasets.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top panel: `Accuracy`\n- Middle panel: `F1 Score`\n- Bottom panel: `AUROC`\n\nX-axis labels:\n- `Tabula Sapiens`\n- `Tabula Sapiens (20 common cell types)`\n- `Pancreas`\n- `ImmGen`\n- `Asian Immune Diversity Atlas`\n\nLegend/models:\n- `scGPT (unfrozen, with pseudo-bulks)`\n- `scGPT (frozen, with pseudo-bulks & single cells)`\n- `scGPT (frozen, with pseudo-bulks)`\n- `CellWhisperer (scGPT-based)`\n- `CellAssign (same as in Fig. 2c)`\n\nAdditional annotations:\n- A bracket labels the first four model variants as `Fine-tuned`.\n- A bracket labels `CellAssign` as `Zero-shot`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell or cell-type datasets are referenced by name, including Tabula Sapiens, Pancreas, ImmGen, and Asian Immune Diversity Atlas.\n- The figure concerns cell type classification or annotation performance, inferred from dataset names and model labels, but no cells, tissues, microscopy images, or molecular structures are visually shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The compared approaches include scGPT variants using `pseudo-bulks`, `single cells`, frozen versus unfrozen settings, a CellWhisperer scGPT-based model, and CellAssign in a zero-shot setting.\n\nFindings visible from the plotted values:\n- AUROC is generally high for most scGPT and CellWhisperer variants, often around 0.90-0.98.\n- CellAssign performs lower on several datasets, especially Tabula Sapiens AUROC around 0.52 and F1 around 0.12.\n- Accuracy and F1 are lower and more variable than AUROC across datasets.\n- Asian Immune Diversity Atlas shows the highest visible accuracy values, with CellAssign around 0.83 and CellWhisperer around 0.79."]}, {"figure_index": 15, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Correspondence between CellWhisperer and GSVA scores", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a grouped bar-chart scientific figure comparing model performance across datasets.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Three stacked metric panels sharing the same x-axis:\n  - Top: `Accuracy`\n  - Middle: `F1 Score`\n  - Bottom: `AUROC`\n- X-axis datasets/categories:\n  - `Tabula Sapiens`\n  - `Tabula Sapiens (20 common cell types)`\n  - `Pancreas`\n  - `ImmGen`\n  - `Asian Immune Diversity Atlas`\n- Legend/model conditions:\n  - `UCE (unfrozen, with pseudo-bulks)`\n  - `UCE (frozen, with pseudo-bulks & single cells)`\n  - `UCE (frozen, with pseudo-bulks)`\n  - `CellWhisperer (UCE-based)`\n  - `CellAssign (same as in Fig. 2c)`\n- Side annotation groups models as:\n  - `Fine-tuned`\n  - `Zero-shot`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell / cell-type annotation benchmark datasets are shown, including human Tabula Sapiens, pancreas, mouse immune `ImmGen`, and Asian Immune Diversity Atlas.\n- The plotted task appears to compare cell-type classification or annotation performance across these datasets.\n\nFindings visible from the bars:\n- `CellWhisperer (UCE-based)` has the highest or near-highest performance for `ImmGen`, with values around `0.97` accuracy, `0.95` F1, and `0.99` AUROC.\n- `Tabula Sapiens` performance is low for all models, especially `CellAssign`, with accuracy around `0.02` and AUROC around `0.52`.\n- `Tabula Sapiens (20 common cell types)` shows improved results relative to full Tabula Sapiens.\n- AUROC is generally high across most datasets and models except full Tabula Sapiens.\n- The figure compares fine-tuned UCE variants against zero-shot CellWhisperer and CellAssign baselines."]}, {"figure_index": 16, "page_no": 18, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about evaluating gene-set associations in the Human Disease dataset.\n\nVisible content:\n- Left schematic panel: A workflow starts with a “Gene set name” input, shown as “Colorectal cancer,” and “Associated genes,” shown as “APC, MSH2, ...”.\n- Two model/analysis interfaces are compared:\n  - “CellWhisperer term scoring,” producing an embedding similarity score.\n  - “Gene set variation analysis (GSVA),” using selected and all other samples with example genes APC and MSH2.\n- The dataset label on the side reads “Human Disease dataset (n=14112).”\n- The analysis is described as “Tested on 8812 gene sets from 6 libraries.”\n\nQuantitative panels:\n- Bottom-left scatter plot compares “GSVA score for gene set” on the x-axis against “CellWhisperer score” on the y-axis. It reports “Pearson correlation: 0.52.”\n- Bottom-middle line plot compares signed KS statistics using cumulative proportion curves for CellWhisperer scores, with negative and positive curves. It reports “Signed KS-statistic: 0.5.”\n\nMain results panel:\n- A set of violin/box-like distributions shows “Signed KS-statistic” across gene set libraries.\n- Libraries listed include:\n  - Panglao DB Augmented 2021\n  - Tabula Sapiens\n  - Azimuth 2023\n  - DisGeNET\n  - GO Biological Process 2023\n  - GO Molecular Function 2023\n- The x-axis indicates correspondence from negative to positive.\n- A column reports “Number of significant terms,” including values such as 164 of 178, 403 of 469, 1279 of 1425, 142 of 187, 4083 of 5406, and 792 of 1147.\n\nRight panel:\n- A cumulative proportion plot shows correlation coefficient rho on the x-axis and cumulative proportion on the y-axis.\n- The plot is annotated with example terms including “colorectal cancer,” “thyroid carcinoma,” “nephropathy,” “stature,” and “dyslexia.”\n- A bracket/arrow indicates correlation direction from negative to positive.\n- The panel notes n=187.\n\nOverall finding shown:\nThe figure presents a comparison between CellWhisperer term scoring and GSVA across disease and gene ontology gene-set libraries, showing generally positive correspondence and many statistically significant gene-set terms."]}, {"figure_index": 17, "page_no": 18, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific figure titled “Batch integration score” with two visible sections separated by a vertical divider.\n\nLeft panel: “Concept”\n- Shows a schematic scatter plot of three batches.\n- Labels: “Low (poor)” on the left and “High (good)” on the right.\n- Three colored point groups are labeled:\n  - Batch 1 in light blue\n  - Batch 2 in orange\n  - Batch 3 in teal\n- The low/poor example shows batch-separated clusters.\n- The high/good example shows mixed batches, indicating better batch integration.\n\nRight panel: “Performance”\n- Horizontal bar chart comparing embedding methods across datasets or tasks.\n- Legend:\n  - gray bars: “scFM embeddings”\n  - orange bars: “CellWhisperer embeddings”\n- Y-axis labels:\n  - Geneformer\n  - scGPT\n  - UCE\n- X-axis label: “Average Silhouette Width score”\n- The scale runs approximately from 0.0 to 0.8+.\n- Orange CellWhisperer bars are slightly longer than gray scFM bars for the shown rows, indicating higher batch integration scores in this comparison.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The figure concerns single-cell foundation model embeddings or single-cell data integration, but no actual cells, tissues, genes, or microscopy images are shown.\n- The biological objects are represented abstractly as colored batch points in embedding space.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data are represented as embeddings from models including Geneformer, scGPT, UCE, and CellWhisperer.\n- The metric shown is Average Silhouette Width score for batch integration.\n\nFinding:\n- Better batch integration is illustrated as mixing of batch-colored points.\n- CellWhisperer embeddings appear to achieve higher average silhouette width scores than scFM embeddings across Geneformer, scGPT, and UCE comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 18, "page_no": 18, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel schematic and quantitative result figure.\n\nLeft panel, labeled “Concept”: shows a conceptual embedding-space diagram with point clusters. It contrasts “Low (poor)” and “High (good)” performance. Colored dots represent cells or embeddings, with labels “Cell type 1,” “Cell type 2,” and “Cell type 3.” The low-performance side shows mixed cell-type colors in overlapping clusters, while the high-performance side shows more separated clusters by cell type.\n\nRight panel, labeled “Performance”: horizontal bar chart comparing two embedding sources:\n- gray: “scFM embeddings”\n- orange: “CellWhisperer embeddings”\n\nRows/models shown on the y-axis: Geneformer, scGPT, and UCE. The x-axis is “Average Bio score,” ranging roughly from 0.0 to 0.5+. Orange CellWhisperer embedding bars are slightly longer than gray scFM embedding bars for each listed model, indicating higher average bio scores.\n\nBiological source objects: cells/cell types represented as colored points; embeddings derived from single-cell foundation models and CellWhisperer.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: cell or model embeddings are evaluated by cell-type separation/biological score; comparison across Geneformer, scGPT, and UCE.\n\nVisible finding: CellWhisperer embeddings outperform or slightly improve over scFM embeddings across the three displayed models by average bio score."]}, {"figure_index": 19, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 2 | See next page for caption.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific workflow/analysis figure about text-embedding variants and CellWhisperer scoring.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: A workflow box titled “Annotation queries” containing four colored query examples:\n  - “The embryonic stage begins with a fertilized oocyte [...]”\n  - “The blastula stage begins with the loss of the zona [...]”\n  - “During the gastrula stage the extraembryonic mesoderm [...]”\n  - “During organogenesis somites progressively form neural [...]”\n- An arrow labeled “LLM” transforms these annotation queries into a lower box titled “Query variants”, showing multiple colored dot variants and example generated paraphrases such as:\n  - “Upon fertilization, an oocyte that contains [...]”\n  - “The onset of the embryonic stage is marked by [...]”\n- A rightward arrow labeled “CellWhisperer embedding model” leads to a scatter plot titled “densMAP of variant text embeddings”.\n- Middle panel: densMAP scatter plot with axes “UMAP 1” and “UMAP 2”. Points are colored by query group; visible clusters separate red/yellow points in the upper-right, cyan points lower-middle, and purple points lower-left.\n- Another rightward arrow labeled “CellWhisperer scoring of Human Development dataset” leads to a heatmap.\n- Right panel: Heatmap titled “Pairwise correlation across all cells in the Human Development dataset”. It shows a clustered correlation matrix with dendrograms on the left and bottom, colored side annotations matching query groups, and a colorbar labeled “Correlation between CellWhisperer scores” ranging approximately from -1 to 1.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- Human Development dataset.\n- Developmental-stage annotation text mentioning embryonic stage, blastula, gastrula, organogenesis, fertilized oocyte, zona, extraembryonic mesoderm, somites, and neural development.\n- The figure does not show biological images or cells directly; it analyzes text-derived scores across cells.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Annotation queries are expanded into LLM-generated query variants.\n- Query variants are embedded with the CellWhisperer embedding model.\n- Variant text embeddings are visualized using densMAP/UMAP.\n- CellWhisperer scores are computed on a Human Development dataset.\n- Pairwise correlations of CellWhisperer scores across all cells are clustered and shown as a heatmap.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Query variants form distinct embedding clusters corresponding to the colored annotation groups.\n- The correlation heatmap shows block structure, indicating that variants within the same or related query groups have similar CellWhisperer score patterns, while some groups are negatively or weakly correlated with others."]}, {"figure_index": 20, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure labeled **a-e** about **CellWhisperer organ scores**, organ marker genes, literature co-mentions, enrichment, and spatial expression.\n\n**Panel a:** Scatterplot grid titled “Correlation of CellWhisperer organ scores and established organ-specific marker gene expression.” Organs shown: cerebrum, heart, intestine, kidney, liver, lung, muscle, pancreas, spleen, stomach. Each subplot compares **CellWhisperer z-score** on the y-axis against **marker gene z-score** on the x-axis, with blue points and pink regression lines. Correlation coefficients and p-values are printed in each panel.\n\n**Panel b:** Violin plot grid titled “CellWhisperer-identified organ marker genes are frequently co-mentioned with the organ in the scientific literature.” Organs: cerebrum, heart, intestine, kidney, liver, lung, muscle, pancreas, spleen, stomach. Y-axis is **Number of papers** on a log-like scale. Violin groups compare **CellWhisperer-identified marker genes**, **marker genes from fetal gene expression atlas**, **shared marker genes**, and **random genes control**. Odds ratios, p-values, and sample sizes are annotated.\n\n**Panel c:** Violin plot grid titled “Papers co-mentioning pairs of organ and gene names identified by CellWhisperer.” Same organ set. X-axis groups expression enrichment categories: **< 0**, **0 - 1.5**, and **> 1.5**, with red brackets and counts labeled as “new.” Y-axis is **Number of papers**. P-values and sample sizes are annotated.\n\n**Panel d:** Dot/bubble plot titled “Gene set enrichment for CellWhisperer-identified ‘heart’ marker genes.” Biological process/pathway terms include striated muscle contraction, myofibril assembly, muscle contraction, actomyosin structure organization, sarcomere organization, heart contraction, regulation of heart contraction, cardiac muscle cell development, heart development, and dilated cardiomyopathy. X-axis is **Combined score**. Dot size encodes fraction of genes in gene set; color encodes adjusted p-value.\n\n**Panel e:** Spatial expression plots titled “Spatial expression of ‘liver’ marker genes in a CS8 embryo.” Four embryo-shaped spatial maps are shown. Established liver marker genes: **HNF4A** and **FGA**. CellWhisperer-identified marker genes: **REEP6** and **GOLT1A**. Red spatial signal overlays gray embryo tissue outlines.\n\nVisible finding: CellWhisperer organ scores correlate with established organ marker expression, CellWhisperer-identified marker genes are enriched for literature co-mention with corresponding organs, heart markers enrich for cardiac/muscle biological processes, and selected liver marker genes show spatial expression in a CS8 embryo."]}, {"figure_index": 21, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic and quantitative evaluation figure for a biomedical/modeling workflow.\n\nVisible content:\n- Left panel: An “Evaluation Conversations dataset (n=200)” shown as stacked dialogue cards. Each sample includes a purple “transcriptome embedding” input, a question bubble reading `Q: \"Describe this sample\"`, and an answer bubble beginning `A: \"These are cancerous cells ...\"`.\n- Middle workflow: Inputs are passed into a model labeled vertically `CellWhisperer`, producing a `Prediction`. A `Ground truth answer` is also shown. Both connect to a `Perplexity` evaluation box.\n- Scatter plot panel: Perplexity scores are plotted, with x-axis labeled `Perplexity lower is better`, ranging approximately from `1.51` to `14.25`. The y-axis appears to show ranked or discrete test cases. Legend distinguishes:\n  - Gray dots: `Mismatched embeddings (n=30)`\n  - Purple dots: `Matched embedding (n=1)`\n- Right-side annotation: A column labeled `Quantile for matched embedding (inverted):` lists values including `0.87`, `0.70`, `1.00`, `0.97`, and `1.00`.\n- Far-right summary panel: A horizontal distribution/box-like plot labeled `Perplexity quantiles for all 200 test cases`, with x-axis `Perplexity quantiles (inverted)` from `0.0` to `1.0`.\n\nBiological source object:\n- Transcriptome embeddings, likely representing gene-expression profiles from biological samples or cells.\n\nModel/interface:\n- A multimodal or embedding-conditioned language model interface labeled `CellWhisperer`, queried with natural language and transcriptome embeddings.\n\nFinding shown:\n- Matched transcriptome embeddings tend to achieve favorable, low perplexity relative to mismatched embeddings, summarized as high inverted perplexity quantiles across 200 test cases."]}, {"figure_index": 22, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a scientific plot panel titled “Recognition of cell types from transcriptomic embeddings” (title partially cropped). It contains horizontal bar plots comparing classification/recognition performance under two mismatch conditions:\n\n- Left column: “Mismatch: Input embedding”\n- Right column: “Mismatch: Output label”\n\nVisible y-axis labels list biological cell types, including:\n- CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell\n- CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell\n- CD8-positive alpha-beta cytokine secreting effector T cell\n- NK cell\n- basal cell\n- basophil\n- capillary endothelial cell\n- classical monocyte\n- club cell\n- erythrocyte\n- hepatocyte\n- macrophage\n- memory B cell\n- monocyte\n- naive B cell\n- neutrophil\n- non-classical monocyte\n- respiratory goblet cell\n- type II pneumocyte\n\nEach row has green horizontal bars with dark outlines and small error bars/markers, plotted against a “Quantile” x-axis. Vertical reference lines appear around mid/high quantile values.\n\nAt the bottom, a summary row labeled “All 177 cell types” shows violin/diamond-like distribution summaries for both mismatch conditions, also on a “Quantile” axis.\n\nBiological source objects: human/animal cell types inferred from transcriptomic embeddings; specific organism is not visible in the cropped image.\n\nModel/interface details visible: comparison between input embedding mismatch and output label mismatch, implying a model or embedding-based classifier evaluated across cell-type labels.\n\nVisible finding: most listed cell types have high quantile values close to the upper end of the scale in the input-embedding mismatch condition; output-label mismatch appears more variable for some cell types."]}, {"figure_index": 23, "page_no": 22, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains two side-by-side violin plot panels comparing language model perplexity.\n\nLeft panel: titled “Cell Type Conversations dataset (n=15,158)”. The y-axis is labeled “← log₂ perplexity”. The x-axis has four model/input conditions: “Mistral (7B)”, “Llama 3.3 (70B)”, “CellWhisperer (7B)”, and “CellWhisperer (7B)”. A legend labeled “Input” indicates three input types: “Top 50 genes” in blue, “Transcriptome embedding” in green, and “Both” in combined blue/green. The visible result shows Mistral with the highest perplexity distribution, Llama lower, and CellWhisperer conditions lower still.\n\nRight panel: titled “Evaluation Conversations dataset (n=200)”. It uses the same y-axis label and four x-axis conditions. Mistral and Llama show higher perplexity distributions than the CellWhisperer conditions. CellWhisperer with transcriptome embedding and CellWhisperer with both inputs show the lowest perplexity ranges.\n\nBiological source objects: the figure refers to cell type conversations and transcriptome-derived inputs, including top 50 genes and transcriptome embeddings. No specific cell types, organisms, genes, assays, or datasets beyond the panel titles are visible.\n\nModel interfaces/transformations: transcriptome information appears to be transformed into either top-gene text/features, transcriptome embeddings, or both, then evaluated as input to language models including Mistral, Llama 3.3, and CellWhisperer.\n\nFinding shown: CellWhisperer input configurations have visibly lower log₂ perplexity than general language models, especially compared with Mistral, across both datasets."]}]

