 Wang, Shaopan Xiong, Gengru Chen, Wei Gao, Sheng Guo, Yancheng He, Ju Huang, Jiaheng Liu, Zhendong Li, Xiaoyang Li, et al. Reinforcement learning optimization for largescale learning: An efficient and user-friendly scaling library. arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.06122 , 2025.\n- [60] Zihe Liu, Jiashun Liu, Yancheng He, Weixun Wang, Jiaheng Liu, Ling Pan, Xinyu Hu, Shaopan Xiong, Ju Huang, Jian Hu, et al. Part i: Tricks or traps? a deep dive into rl for llm reasoning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.08221 , 2025.\n- [61] Hardy Chen, Haoqin Tu, Fali Wang, Hui Liu, Xianfeng Tang, Xinya Du, Yuyin Zhou, and Ci660 hang Xie. Sft or rl? an early investigation into training r1-like reasoning large vision-language 661 models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.11468 , 2025. 662\n\n- [62] Wenhao Zhang, Yuexiang Xie, Yuchang Sun, Yanxi Chen, Guoyin Wang, Yaliang Li, Bolin Ding, 663 and Jingren Zhou. On-policy rl meets off-policy experts: Harmonizing supervised fine-tuning 664 and reinforcement learning via dynamic weighting. arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.11408 , 2025. 665\n\n666\n\n667\n\n668\n\n669\n\n670\n\n671\n\n672\n\n673\n\n674\n\n675\n\n676\n\n677\n\n678\n\n679\n\n680\n\n681\n\n682\n\n683\n\n684\n\n685\n\n686\n\n687\n\n688\n\n689\n\n690\n\n691\n\n692\n\n693\n\n694\n\n695\n\n696\n\n697\n\n698\n\n699\n\n700\n\n701\n\n702\n\n703\n\n704\n\n## A. Prompt list\n\nThe prompt used for the Pathology VQA is:\n\nYour task: 1. Think through the question step by step, enclose your reasoning process in &lt;think&gt;...&lt;/think&gt; tags. 2. Then provide the correct single-letter choice (A, B, C, D,...) inside &lt;answer&gt;...&lt;/answer&gt; tags. 3. No extra information or text outside of these tags.\n\nThe prompt used for the self-verifier is:\n\nYou are an expert in pathology. You are given a QUESTION and a PROPOSED SOLUTION. Your job is to: 1. Break down each component of the proposed solution. 2. Think step by step to verify if the proposed solution is correct given the question and the figure. 3. Write a line of the form \"The proposed solution is correct\" or \"The proposed solution is incorrect\" at the end of your response based on your analysis. QUESTION: question. PROPOSED SOLUTION: solution.\n\nThe prompt used for the self-corrector is:\n\nYou are also given a question and an analysis for the question. Your job is to outline your step-by-step thought process for deriving a correct solution and also write down the correct solution. Using this format: &lt;think&gt; Your step-by-step reasoning of the question and solution &lt; / think&gt;&lt;answer&gt; Your final answer &lt; / answer&gt; Question: question Solution: out\\_verifier.\n\nThe prompt used for the reason corrector is:\n\nYou are given QUESTION, REASON, and SOLUTION. Your task is to correct the REASON and SOLUTION. QUESTION: question. SOLUTION: solution. REASON: reason The REASON is WRONG. Your solution:\n\nThe prompts used for training TeamPath for caption summary include:\n\nProvide a concise pathological summary of the tissue shown in this histopathology image, highlighting any abnormal cellular or structural features in one paragraph. Based on the visual characteristics in this image, summarize the likely histological diagnosis and key indicators leading to it in one paragraph. Describe the main histopathological patterns visible in this image and summarize what they suggest about the tissue state in one paragraph. Summarize the key morphological findings in this histopathology image, including any signs of malignancy, inflammation, or necrosis in one paragraph. Generate a pathology report-style summary based solely on this histological section, mentioning tissue type, grade, and diagnostic clues in one paragraph. Briefly summarize the clinical implications of the abnormalities visible in this histopathology image in one paragraph. From this histopathology image, extract and summarize the most diagnostically relevant features in one paragraph. Identify and summarize any histopathological hallmarks (e.g., mitotic figures, glandular formation, stromal invasion) present in the image in one paragraph. Write a summary suitable for a pathology trainee explaining what this histopathology image represents and why in one paragraph. Provide an expert-level summary of the pathological findings in this histopathology image, including your confidence in the assessment in one paragraph.\n\nThe prompts used for training TeamPath for cross-modality generation (using IDC as an example) include:\n\n722\n\n723\n\n724\n\n725\n\n726\n\n727\n\n728\n\n729\n\n730\n\n731\n\n732\n\n733\n\n734\n\n735\n\n736\n\n737\n\n738\n\n739\n\n740\n\n741\n\n742\n\n743\n\n744\n\nGenerate a list of 100 genes in order of descending expression from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Produce a list of 100 gene names in descending order of expression which represent the expressed genes from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Create a ranked list of 100 genes in decreasing order of expression from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, List the top 100 expressed genes from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Identify the highest expressed 100 genes in decreasing order of expression from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Enumerate a list of 100 genes in descending order of expression from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Compile a descending order list of 100 expressed genes from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Present a sequence of 100 genes ordered by decreasing expression level from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Generate an ordered list of 100 genes by decreasing expression level from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:, Assemble a list of 100 genes from highest to lowest expression from one spot shown in the histopathology image in IDC disease. Cell sentence:\n\n## B. SFT vs RL: Comparison for training strategies.\n\nHow to train a mature reasoning model has always been a controversial topic, as there exist several different strategies and their performances and ranks might vary under different task settings or experiment settings (19, 59, 60). Meanwhile, this kind of discussion has not been investigated in training a large reasoning model for histopathology analysis, and thus, we consider several different approaches to provide an empirical analysis to select and interpret the best combination, which might inspire future directions or different researchers.\n\nWeconduct these experiments based on different base models as well as training strategies. Our base models include Qwen2.5VL-7B, which does not contain domain-specific knowledge and Patho-R1-7B, which contains domain-specific knowledge. We also consider different training strategies, including RL (GRPO), RL (DAPO), SFT, and SFT + RL (GRPO). Extended Data Figures 3 (a) and (b) show that GRPO can achieve a more obvious score improvement while DAPO cannot make an improvement, which implies that a mixture of tricks does not contribute to training a multi-modal pathology reasoning model. Extended Data Figures 3 (c) and (d) show that it is important to select a base model with domain knowledge for training, and performing SFT + RL or direct SFT settings does not benefit this task. Therefore, our optimal choice to build TeamPath for pathology VQA is Patho-R1-7B+RL (GRPO).\n\nIn conclusion, our results showcase that it is important to select a good model with domain knowledge to perform training, and for LMMs that can already possess domain knowledge, directly training them using RL policies can enhance their generalization capabilities without requiring specialized SFT training (also known as cold-start training). Our important findings also align with relevant research across different fields (61, 62), which indirectly validates the reliability of our conclusions.\n\n## C. Studies for training data ablation.\n\nWe also investigate if incorporating more diverse data could help TeamPath for generating a better 745 reasoning path or not. To examine it, we compare the results between only using multi-choice VQA 746 (mc VQA) data and using both multi-choice VQA and open-ended VQA (full VQA) data. Based on our 747 evaluation results shown in Extended Data Figure 4 with validation from the PathMMU dataset, using 748\n\nfull VQA data cannot boost TeamPath's performances in generalizing the results for solving questions 749 in the PathMMU dataset. Therefore, our optimal setting only takes mc VQA for RL training. Details 750 of the reward design for these two different types of data are explained in the Method section. 751\n\n## D. Supplementary figures 752\n\nExtended Data Fig. 1: A flowchart of data-preprocessing used to train TeamPath.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nWorkflow/flowchart figure with two panels labeled **a** and **b**. No axes or legend are present. Panel **a** shows a pipeline for histopathology image processing: downloading TCGA whole-slide images (**9,568**), matching with PathGen 1.6M annotations (**5,650**), processing to extract ROIs (**1,620,876**), and matching ROIs with paired question-answer sets (**200,899**). Side labels indicate outputs/tasks: **Reasoning-driven Summary** and **Reasoning-driven VQA AI-Physician Collaboration**.\n\nPanel **b** shows a spatial transcriptomics generation workflow for two datasets: IDC and Brain. IDC: extract ROIs with 224 radius (**44,974**) then pair radius with transcriptomic data after filtering (**35,536**). Brain: extract ROIs with 224 radius (**47,443**) then pair ROI with transcriptomic data after filtering (**47,443**). Main finding shown is dataset construction counts after matching/filtering for histopathology QA/summary and spatial transcriptomic pairing workflows.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nComposite evaluation figure for biomedical visual question answering. It shows a small immunohistochemistry microscopy image at top left, a question about which marker is targeted by the brown staining pattern, multiple boxed model responses labeled Patho-R1, MedGemma-4B, Qwen2.5VL-7B, MedVLThinker-7B, InternVL3-8B, PathGen-LLaVA, and TeamPath-7B, plus an annotated answer: “(C) Synaptophysin.”\n\nGreen text indicates correct information and red text indicates wrong information. Most model boxes are marked with red X icons for incorrect reasoning or answers, while the TeamPath-7B response at the bottom is marked correct with green highlighting. The figure’s main finding is a qualitative comparison of model reasoning accuracy on a pathology IHC image question, showing TeamPath-7B producing the correct answer while several other vision-language models fail.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 2: Case study (topic: lipoblast, which is a protein found in the presynaptic vesicles of neurons and neuroendocrine cells that plays a role in synaptic transmission) based on the outputs from different models. We highlight the correct information with green text and incorrect information with red text. For the models with errors, we consider two cases. The first case is a wrong answer, the second case is a confused reasoning path.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 3: Training strategies optimization with different settings. The results are evaluated based on the PathMMU dataset. (a) Accuracy across different categories based on the base model and different RL strategies. (b) Accuracy and rank across different categories based on the base model and different RL strategies. (c) Accuracy across different categories based on different base models and different RL/SFT strategies. (d) Accuracy and rank across different categories based on different base models and different RL/SFT strategies.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel experimental results figure with bar charts and bubble/scatter plots comparing biomedical model performance across datasets.\n\nPanels:\n- **a**: Bar chart with y-axis roughly 40-80, x-axis datasets **PubMed, SocialPath, Atlas, EduContent, PathCLS, Avg**. Legend compares **Patho-R1-7B+RL (GRPO)**, **Patho-R1-7B**, and **Patho-R1-7B+RL (DAPO)**.\n- **b**: Bubble plot for the same datasets, with rows for the three Patho-R1 variants. Bubble size/color encode performance.\n- **c**: Bar chart comparing multiple models, including **Qwen2.5VL-7B+SFT**, **Qwen2.5VL-7B+SFT+RL (GRPO)**, **Qwen2.5VL-7B**, **Qwen2.5VL-7B+RL**, **Patho-R1-7B+RL (GRPO)**, and **Patho-R1-7B**.\n- **d**: Bubble plot comparing the same model variants as panel c across the same datasets.\n\nMeasured quantity appears to be benchmark performance/accuracy-like scores across biomedical/pathology-related datasets. Main visible finding: **Patho-R1-7B+RL (GRPO)** generally shows the strongest or near-strongest performance, especially in panels a/b, while reinforcement-learning variants improve over several baselines across the average and most datasets.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nBar chart comparing RL training data variants across benchmark categories. The title is “Comparisons between data used for RL training.” The x-axis categories are PubMed, SocialPath, Atlas, EduContent, PathCLS, and Avg. The y-axis ranges roughly from 40 to 80. Legend shows green bars for “mc VQA” and blue bars for “full VQA.”\n\nThe plotted metric is not explicitly named, but values appear to be performance scores/percentages. Main finding: mc VQA and full VQA are similar on PubMed, SocialPath, Atlas, and EduContent, while mc VQA is much higher on PathCLS and higher on the average.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 4: Comparisons for the PathMMU VQA question set with different training data. The metric is accuracy.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 5: An example of using TeamPath to correct the reasoning path from pathologists.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nComposite figure with a histology/microscopy panel and annotated QA comparison. Left panel shows an H&E-stained tissue section with epithelial structures/tubules. Right panels contain text boxes comparing an “Expert answer” versus a “TeamPath answer,” with green text marking correct information and red text marking wrong information. The question asks about the tubular lining structure, and the highlighted finding is that the cells have cuticular/brush borders suggesting specialized renal proximal tubule epithelium.\n\n##### Ablation studies for the choices of verifier\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nGrouped bar chart comparing four methods across datasets/tasks. The x-axis labels are PubMed, SocialPath, EduContent, PathCLS, Atlas, and Avg. The y-axis ranges from 0 to 1, likely a performance metric. Legend shows Raw, Corrector, o3, and o4-mini.\n\nMain visible finding: o3 and o4-mini generally outperform Raw and Corrector, with o3 reaching 1.0 on Atlas and o4-mini reaching 1.0 on EduContent. Average performance is highest for o4-mini, followed closely by o3, while Raw and Corrector are lower and similar.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 6: Ablation studies of using different models as verifiers.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 7: Examples of model outputs for caption summary tasks. We highlight both correct and incorrect information.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nFigure type: multi-panel qualitative comparison/annotation figure, likely from a biomedical VQA or pathology captioning evaluation.\n\nVisible panels/labels: eight rounded text panels labeled TeamPath, MedGemma-4B, Qwen2.5VL-7B, InternVL3-8B, PathGen-LLaVA-13B, Qwen2.5VL-3B, Patho-R1-7B, and MedVLThinker-7B. A legend at top states yellow text indicates concept-matched context and red text indicates incorrect context.\n\nQuantity shown: textual model outputs describing histopathology/microscopy findings, with highlighted spans marking correct concept matches versus errors.\n\nMain visible finding: model responses vary substantially in pathology interpretation; some outputs contain correct concepts such as spindle-shaped cells, myxoid stroma, or inflammatory infiltrates, while many highlighted red spans indicate hallucinated or incorrect diagnoses/features such as hepatocellular carcinoma, pulmonary fibrosis, cardiac muscle fibers, malignancy, or protozoaceous material.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 8: Extended analyses of spatial transcriptomic generation. (a) GPCC across different methods based on the brain tissue. (b) GPCC across different methods based on the IDC sample. (c) Comparison between finetuned Qwen2.5VL-7B and TeamPath based on the brain tissue. (d) Comparison between finetuned Qwen2.5VL-7B and TeamPath based on the IDC sample.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel bar-plot figure comparing model performance metrics across biomedical/scientific benchmark tasks.\n\nPanels:\n- **a**: Bar chart of **GPCC** across several models, including Teampath-7B, PathGen-LLavA-13B, MedGemma-4B, Qwen2.5VL-3B, Qwen2.5VL-7B, Patho-R1-7B, InternVL3-8B, MedVLThinker-7B, and Cell2Sentence-1B. Teampath-7B has the highest positive GPCC; Qwen2.5VL-7B is visibly negative.\n- **b**: Bar chart of **GPCC** for the same model set. Teampath-7B is much higher than all others; Qwen2.5VL-3B shows a smaller positive value.\n- **c**: Three small bar charts comparing **Teampath-7B** vs **Qwen2.5VL-7B(ft)** on **SPCC**, **MSE**, and **GPCC**. Teampath-7B is higher on SPCC, slightly lower on MSE, and slightly lower on GPCC.\n- **d**: Three small bar charts comparing **Teampath-7B** vs **Qwen2.5VL-7B(ft)** on **SPCC**, **MSE**, and **GPCC**. Teampath-7B is slightly higher on SPCC, similar on MSE, and substantially higher on GPCC.\n\nMain visible finding: **Teampath-7B generally shows stronger correlation-based performance, especially GPCC, than the compared vision-language or biomedical models, with error bars shown for each bar.**\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nComposite biomedical QA/evaluation panel containing a histology microscopy crop and annotated text. The left inset shows H&E-like ovarian cystic tissue/microscopy. The main panel asks about the epithelial lining in a cystic structure, with multiple-choice options and an annotated answer: “Simple ciliated columnar epithelium.” A lower bordered explanation box uses green/red color coding where green indicates correct information and red indicates incorrect information. It critiques a model response labeled “TeamPath-7B,” highlighting that the image is described as serous cystadenoma but the explanation contains conflicting claims about simple cuboidal versus simple ciliated columnar epithelium. No axes or quantitative measurements are shown.\n\nExtended Data Fig. 9: A case study of TeamPath output for pathology VQA with an incorrect reasoning path but a correct answer.\n=== END COMPLETE DOCUMENT ===\n\n=== TARGET SCHEMA ===\n{\n  \"$defs\": {\n    \"InputRouteDiscovery\": {\n      \"description\": \"One evidence-grounded route without imposing a taxonomy label.\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"route_label\": {\n          \"description\": \"Concise document-grounded label for exactly one source-to-model path. Do not create an additional combined or fused route merely because a configuration uses several already-recorded routes together.\",\n          \"title\": \"Route Label\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"model_name\": {\n          \"description\": \"Named generative model receiving the input. Copy the paper's name.\",\n          \"title\": \"Model Name\",\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"lifecycle_phase_verbatim\": {\n          \"description\": \"One lifecycle phase stated by the paper, such as pretraining, instruction tuning, inference, or evaluation. Create separate route records when the same source-to-model path must be represented in different phases. Do not normalize the paper's phase wordin