/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000827_745a8d0e1225/full_2026_07_06_rec_000827_745a8d0e1225_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:243:<![CDATA[To illustrate CellWhisperer's ability to process, organize and annotate large transcriptome datasets, we clustered the CellWhisperer embeddings for 705,430 GEO-derived human transcriptomes and used the CellWhisperer chat model to textually annotate these clusters (Fig. 1b; interactive version at https://cellwhisperer.bocklab.org/geo). The CellWhisperer embeddings successfully captured cell types, developmental stages, tissues, diseases and other cell characteristics. For example, when querying the embedding model with the search term 'infection']]>  </text>
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000827_745a8d0e1225/full_2026_07_06_rec_000827_745a8d0e1225_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:3125:    To investigate whether CellWhisperer can identify temporal dynamics in embryonic development, we prepared queries corresponding to four key developmental stages using LLM-based aggregation of vertebrate embryology descriptions. The CellWhisperer scores for these queries matched the expected timing for these stages (Fig. 3a).
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000827_745a8d0e1225/full_2026_07_06_rec_000827_745a8d0e1225_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:3212:<![CDATA[Fig. 3 | CellWhisperer analysis of organ development based on scRNA-seq datasets of human embryos. a , Overview of the Human Development scRNA-seq dataset with CellWhisperer scores for four key developmental stages. Queries were derived from Carnegie stage annotations using GPT-4o. The heat map shows mean CellWhisperer scores calculated across all cells for each time point. b , CellWhisperer scores for query 'heart'. The average score across all cells was calculated for each time point and then standardized across time points (as z scores). c , Overlap between CellWhisperer-identified marker genes of heart development (brown), previously reported heart-specific markers derived from an atlas of fetal gene expression 32 (dark gray) and randomly selected genes as controls (light gray). Left, Venn diagram with odds ratio and P value (two-sided Fisher's exact test). Right, number of papers per gene that co-mentioned the gene name and the term 'heart' (two-sided Mann-Whitney U -test). For fair comparison, CellWhisperer-derived genes were selected to yield a matching]]>  </text>
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000827_745a8d0e1225/full_2026_07_06_rec_000827_745a8d0e1225_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:3244:- **Panel a:** Heatmap showing “CellWhisperer captures key developmental stages” across **days after fertilization** and queried Carnegie/developmental stages. Rows include **CS1-2, CS3-5, CS6-8, CS9-23**. Columns are days after fertilization. A color scale labeled **CellWhisperer score (mean across cells per day)** ranges from blue/negative to red/positive. Top schematic marks developmental stages: **zygote, blastula, gastrula, organogenesis**. Text snippets at right show example CellWhisperer natural-language queries for embryonic stages.
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_05/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000827_745a8d0e1225/full_2026_07_06_rec_000827_745a8d0e1225_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:3250:Biological source objects visible: embryonic developmental stages, CS8 embryo spatial transcriptomics/expression maps, heart marker genes, fetal gene expression atlas markers, gene-name literature co-mentions.
